/**
@page cosmology_documentation_quality Documentation quality and maintenance

@section quality_contract Required documentation standard

Every maintained C/C++ file and Python module has a purpose and an API/workflow
entry in @ref cosmology_tools. Model APIs state quantities, units, assumptions,
parameters, return values, failure behavior and whether operations are
collective or host/device work. Numerical descriptions identify centering,
Fourier signs, normalization, DC/Nyquist rules, precision and the relation of
the implemented algorithm to cited papers. Deployment scripts retain their
actual command/environment/root constraints rather than hypothetical portability.

The strict builder enables undocumented-member, wrong/incomplete-parameter and
missing-return warnings. Extraction includes private/static helpers so that
missing documentation is not hidden behind EXTRACT_ALL. It emits XML and checks
that required source-level Python callables/classes and all compiled source files
are represented with documentation. CLI dispatch bodies and imported runtime
settings are implementation-only source-browser material, not fictitious module
APIs. Nested local callbacks are implementation details rather than exported
callables; their owning routines and source remain visible.

@section quality_python Python parser normalization

Doxygen 1.14 does not recognize every required bare annotated dataclass field.
The documentation-only filter rewrites such a line to a parser placeholder,
preserving its line count. That transformed text is never imported/executed.
Initializers are hidden to avoid presenting the placeholder as a runtime default;
the source browser shows the original unfiltered declaration and its annotation.
The frozen dataclass and NumPy read-only semantics are documented separately.

@section quality_checks Reproducible checks

The builder records Doxygen version, source roots, extracted counts, warnings,
coverage failures and the output index. A warning or missing required API
contract fails the build. Relevant CMake/CTest checks exercise the documentation
infrastructure and the existing host/MPI physics regressions. C++ comment-only
equivalence and Python AST equality establish that annotation edits have not
changed executable logic. Source byte hashes still change: historical campaigns
must retain the frozen sources they actually hashed.

Offline SVG math is the default when the complete TeX/Ghostscript/PDF-to-SVG toolchain is available.
The MathJax alternative uses the public CDN and needs network access to render
equations in the browser; this dependency is explicit, not an offline guarantee.
Generated HTML is reviewed for formula rendering, readable tables, source links
and navigation. Doxygen's checks do not prove that a formula is physically
correct or a citation applicable; those are reviewed against the implementation,
independent test equations and primary references. API extraction counts are
coverage evidence, not a scientific qualification. No numerical tolerance,
force kernel, time schedule or data-movement rule is changed to improve a
documentation result.

@section quality_upkeep Updating public behavior

When model/numerical/API behavior changes, update the corresponding source
contract and manual formulas, explain conservation/stability/reproducibility
impacts, and add the appropriate regression. Run `cosmology_docs` and review its
warning/coverage reports. Do not change a frozen campaign's recorded failures or
hashes to accommodate newly edited documentation; create a new campaign or use
its original retained source snapshot.
*/
